{"title":"Homework 7","markdown":{"headingText":"Homework 7","headingAttr":{"id":"","classes":["unnumbered"],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n\n## Problem 1\n\nAssume a Poisson($\\mu$) model for the number of home runs hit (in total by both teams) in a MLB game.\nLet $X_1, \\ldots, X_n$ be a random sample of home run counts for  $n$ games.\n\nSuppose we want to estimate $\\theta = \\mu e^{-\\mu}$, the probability that any single game has exactly 1 HR (for Poisson($\\mu$), $P(X = 1) = e^{-\\mu}\\,u^1/1! = \\mu e^{-\\mu}$).\nConsider two estimators of $\\theta$:\n\n-   $\\hat{\\theta} = \\bar{X} e^{-\\bar{X}}$\n-   $\\hat{p} =\\text{sample proportion of 1s} = \\frac{\\text{number of games in the sample with 1 HR}}{\\text{sample size}}$\n\na.  Compute the value of $\\hat{\\theta}$ based on the sample (3, 0, 1, 4, 0). Write a clearly worded sentence reporting in context this estimate of $\\theta$.\na.  Compute the value of $\\hat{p}$ based on the sample (3, 0, 1, 4, 0).\nWrite a clearly worded sentence reporting in context your estimate of $\\theta$.\na.  Which of these two estimators is the MLE of $\\theta$ in this situation? Explain, without doing any calculations.\na.  It can be shown that $\\hat{p}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\theta$.\nExplain in words what this means.\na.  Is $\\hat{\\theta}$ an unbiased estimator of $\\theta$?\nExplain.\n(You don't have to derive anything; just apply a general principle.)\na.  Suppose $\\mu = 2.3$ and $n=5$.\nExplain in full detail how you would use simulation to approximate the bias of $\\hat{\\theta}$ in this case.\na.  **Coding required.**\nConduct the simulation from the previous part and approximate bias of $\\hat{\\theta}$ when $\\mu = 2.3$ and $n = 5$.\na.  Explain in full detail how you would use simulation to approximate the bias *function* of $\\hat{\\theta}$ when $n=5$.\na.  **Coding required.**\nConduct the simulation from the previous part and plot the approximate bias function when $n=5$.\nFor what values of $\\mu$ does $\\hat{\\theta}$ tend to overestimate $\\mu$? Underestimate?\nFor what values of $\\mu$ is the bias the worst?\n\n\n\n## Problem 2\n\nContinuing Problem 1.\n\na.  It can be shown that $\\text{Var}(\\hat{p}) = \\frac{\\theta(1-\\theta)}{n}$. Compute  $\\text{Var}(\\hat{p})$ when $\\mu = 2.3$ and $n=5$.\nThen write a clearly worded sentence interpreting this value.\na.  Suppose $\\mu = 2.3$ and $n=5$.\nExplain in full detail how you would use simulation to approximate the variance of $\\hat{\\theta}$.\na.  **Coding required.**\nConduct the simulation from the previous part and approximate  the variance of $\\hat{\\theta}$ when $\\mu = 2.3$ and $n=5$.\nThen write a clearly worded sentence interpreting this value.\na.  Which estimator has smaller variance when $\\mu = 2.3$ (and $n=5$)?\nAnswer, but then explain why this information alone is not really useful.\na.  Explain in full detail how you would use simulation to approximate the variance function of $\\hat{\\theta}$ (if $n=5$).\na.  **Coding required.**\nConduct the simulation from the previous part and plot the approximate variance function.\nCompare to the variance function of $\\hat{p}$.\nBased on variability alone, which estimator is preferred?\n\n\n## Problem 3\n\nContinuing Problems 1 and 2\n\na.  Compute $\\text{MSE}(\\hat{p})$ when $\\mu = 2.3$ and $n=5$.\n(You can do the next part first if you want, but it helps to work with specific numbers first.)\na.  Derive the MSE function of $\\hat{p}$.\n(Hint: use facts from previous parts.)\na.  Suppose $\\mu = 2.3$ (and $n=5$).\nExplain in full detail how you would use simulation to approximate the MSE of $\\hat{\\theta}$.\na.  **Coding required.**\nConduct the simulation from the previous part and approximate the MSE of $\\hat{\\theta}$ when $\\mu =2.3$ (and $n=5$).\na.  Which estimator has smaller MSE when $\\mu = 2.3$ (and $n=5$)?\nAnswer, but then explain why this information alone is not really useful.\na.  Explain in full detail how you would use simulation to approximate the MSE *function* of $\\hat{\\theta}$ (if $n=5$).\na.  **Coding required.**\nConduct the simulation from the previous part and plot the approximate MSE function.\nCompare to the MSE function of $\\hat{p}$.\na.  Compare the MSEs of the two estimators for $n=5$ and a few other values of $n$.\nIs there a clear preference between these two estimators? Discuss.\n\n\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","output-file":"hw-07.html"},"language":{"toc-title-document":"Table of contents","toc-title-website":"On this page","related-formats-title":"Other Formats","related-notebooks-title":"Notebooks","source-notebooks-